        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "Dashboard: apps layer — full-bleed tabs + widget gallery",
        "body": "# Dashboard: apps layer — full-bleed tabs + widget gallery\n\n> Two steps toward \"every tab is a web app\": a tab can be a single full-bleed widget, and widgets can be browsed and installed from a registry — through the same approval gate and sandbox as any custom widget.\n\nCloses #101148 and #101149. **Stacked on #101098** — targets `main` with a cumulative diff; review the full-bleed layout + gallery pieces as the delta.\n\n## What's in this PR\n- **Full-bleed tab (#101148)** — a tab may set `layout: \"full\"`, rendering a single widget filling the content area with no grid chrome; a menu toggle flips it. A custom widget can now *be* the tab — a real app surface.\n- **Widget gallery + install-from-URL (#101149)** — browse a registry `index.json`, see the capabilities a widget requests, and install it. The **browser** fetches the index + bundle and sends only the fetched `{ name, manifest, files }` to a new `dashboard.widget.install` method (512 KB / 64-file caps).\n\n## Security (gallery)\nThe gallery is the third-party-code surface, so it's deliberately conservative: **install is client-fetch only** — the gateway never receives or fetches a URL (a source-scan guard test asserts no `fetch`/`https` in the install path), so there is no server-side SSRF; the bundle is capped (512 KB, ≤64 files), path-jailed, extension-allowlisted, and run through the existing `validateWidgetManifest`; requested capabilities are surfaced *before* install. Installing never auto-approves and never bypasses the sandbox — an installed widget lands `pending`, passes the existing operator-approval gate (#101098), and runs in the same `allow-scripts`, opaque-origin, `connect-src 'none'` iframe as any custom widget.\n\n## Real behavior proof\n**Behavior or issue addressed:** The dashboard couldn't express a full app-per-tab, and there was no supply side for widgets (everything hand-authored). This adds a full-bleed tab layout and an approval-gated, sandboxed install-from-registry path.\n**Evidence:** `node scripts/test-projects.mjs extensions/dashboard` → 92 passed (install caps, path jail, `pending` status, and a no-server-fetch source guard); `pnpm vitest run src/lib/dashboard src/pages/plugin src/components` → 299 passed (gallery client-fetch + install, full-bleed render/toggle); `ui:i18n:check`, `ui:build`, and full type-aware oxlint clean. The 3 failures in the full suite are the pre-existing `terminal-panel` browser-fixture flake, unrelated to this change.\n",
